 and $\\sqrt{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248927} {:answer/id #uuid "ffe41db8-dff4-598f-8f14-5e33cb612563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $2\\sqrt{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffe41db8-dff4-598f-8f14-5e33cb612563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $2\\sqrt{7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249664} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb465769-f76a-5b09-871d-27ae5361defb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250021}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0af912c0-685f-5b27-b24c-c0de6bcff1d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-115132" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{7}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=7.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=7,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{7}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=4,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{7}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{7}=2\\sqrt{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab7bfc4-fe0b-5271-b8ca-448cc2f51cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ and $10$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247805} {:answer/id #uuid "515c4642-4ca5-5554-a9a2-a99042231e23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $2.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248154} {:ans