mi-minor axis has length $a=2.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e7bd1b3-9829-5473-8614-8c430afd35b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343033} {:answer/id #uuid "6886ac93-669b-567a-bcd2-cdc962ddc459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343131} {:answer/id #uuid "84a8edc1-a88f-5434-afb0-143e81012a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343372} {:answer/id #uuid "abe395a2-a154-5294-8225-b25996255c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343751} {:answer/id #uuid "d30a39f9-5218-5a0d-9fba-e6226fea77be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abe395a2-a154-5294-8225-b25996255c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343751} :answer-field/id #uuid "59951fee-072e-57e6-a856-742c18a4ab71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344644}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4e0a287-1212-5e98-875c-b8a10ba64112" :question/math-academy-id "q-7595" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=9.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be  $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=9,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=4,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=3.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 3=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345157 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b4bbdd7-3b6a-517b-9ed6-1c446dcec71a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342857} {:answer/id #uuid "74e98528-55f2-5075-9918-6944dd0fae8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25$ and $16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343250} {:answer/id #uuid "93976b13-3f0e-58d6-b564-21e806715d8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{5}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343531} {:answer/id #uuid "d72c0683-cce5-5c5b-b1ee-f4a799dd53cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344126} {:answer/id #uuid "dc66f534-da0e-5146-9fee-e309f47a07d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$50$ and $32$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d72c0683-cce5-5c5b-b1ee-f4a799dd53cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344126} :answer-field/id #uuid "59484f16-7ef8-58ef-819d-3d6908e8ec4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344643}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "548834cd-5f39-5629-b131-aad9f4ac51b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-7601" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=25.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=25,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=5,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 5=10,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0b4d7d8-6b13-51b3-8c16-f1f796b0790a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0b4d7d8-6b13-51b3-8c16-f1f796b0790a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370022} :answer-field/id #uuid "1784709b-8c12-545a-96ba-dc167b20f7c0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370021} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e70a177c-e321-5d97-ad0f-2390d9f8f894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e70a177c-e321-5d97-ad0f-2390d9f8f894" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370024} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c229291-398d-5b8b-8d76-841b2f6ef86c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370023}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a5d8fed-b754-51cf-933f-f6f47cc1ecb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-279198" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{24}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=8$ and ${b}^{2}=24.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=8,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=24,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{24}=2\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{6},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{6}=4\\sqrt{6},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396120 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2368068-c473-5919-aff6-53a14b246b5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396122} {:answer/id #uuid "801b88de-1112-5c33-bd1b-ddb864de2c6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396123} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd49079-798b-58f7-8490-cd6fb613a033" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396124} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b72ba2-9df1-5acc-b385-173c4e36f9a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$18$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396125} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6ef04e-258f-5d68-a139-8ed8a39c2ddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{2}$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c6ef04e-258f-5d68-a139-8ed8a39c2ddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{2}$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396126} :answer-field/id #uuid "bef4ba21-7e9c-5949-8c09-0a45d37f8298" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396121}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "480a616c-8c80-5f81-b3d5-aa7488c0665b" :question/math-academy-id "q-7596" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{18}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=9$ and ${b}^{2}=18.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=9,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=18,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=3\\sqrt{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=3.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}=6\\sqrt{2},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 3=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcb6c4d2-fc5f-560f-9373-4f7a33529527" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{5}$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396129} {:answer/id #uuid "6565345d-7cca-5723-8a4a-06b781ec17a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{5}$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396130} {:answer/id #uuid "71576bd8-08e5-58e9-999c-df3860cd2885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ and $16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396131} {:answer/id #uuid "b48bdeab-9afb-508b-a31f-840d2716535b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396132} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd4bf3f-6eb6-53a8-9729-d9955e874477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{5}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcb6c4d2-fc5f-560f-9373-4f7a33529527" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{5}$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396129} :answer-field/id #uuid "abca09ba-0b0a-5885-90fb-fd1d7d5af56c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396128}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e572cb8d-0721-59ec-897a-5e0fa685feb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-7597" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{20}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=20$ and ${b}^{2}=16.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=20,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=16,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{5},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}=4\\sqrt{5},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 4=8.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399850 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4ffa0ff-df0b-5bc5-b896-3f6772a35019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$28$ and $24$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399852} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f1177f-3e11-593f-ad9c-dda3299fba50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$24.5$ and $18$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399853} {:answer/id #uuid "e59ee665-26a4-5d2e-8c55-26143f2480d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ and $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399854} {:answer/id #uuid "ee829b75-97f1-545e-8cf4-3704340f4ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$49$ and $36$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399855} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e390e1-9f85-5321-a954-3f147b97e82b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e59ee665-26a4-5d2e-8c55-26143f2480d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ and $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399854} :answer-field/id #uuid "44102d96-20a4-51fc-add2-7a7a026df6ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399851}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3113828e-6ca6-530b-804d-2cdc91aade73" :question/math-academy-id "q-115134" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=49$ and ${b}^{2}=36.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=49,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=36,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=6.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 6=12.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401537 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "755a881c-6b14-5e96-bd68-6af58d5ef813" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401539} {:answer/id #uuid "5965e390-56a0-55ef-a7dc-eff34c09cc53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401540} {:answer/id #uuid "1f629da2-c549-5180-8903-3a78b8767616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401541} {:answer/id #uuid "1c1fc5c2-bdfa-52e9-a59e-c6227d171448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401542} {:answer/id #uuid "47a16fa9-9d49-55d0-a143-c2b6cca5c4dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f629da2-c549-5180-8903-3a78b8767616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401541} :answer-field/id #uuid "b41dd28d-0ca0-5222-9cfe-700ddaa37f17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401538}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e791f5b6-5e18-534a-99e5-45cc16c1809f" :question/math-academy-id "q-16455" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=1$ and ${b}^{2}=8.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=1,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{1}=1.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=8,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=1.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 1=2.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087543 :question/id #uuid "e429a65a-d65d-5b3f-a054-e3ab8a61caf3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5299" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x^2-x-6}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x .$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)(x-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x = -2$ and $x = 3.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x = -2$ and $x = 3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048774 :topic/math-academy-id 375 :topic/title "Solving Quadratic Equations by Factoring"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049832 :topic/math-academy-id 1422 :topic/title "Solving Quadratic Equations with Leading Coefficients by Factoring"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bf54f4a-6fe0-5828-91b7-9a1f23658966" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-2, x = 0, x = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085639} {:answer/id #uuid "bc557aaf-c556-5e9b-a479-3e86966eca1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-2, x = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085640} {:answer/id #uuid "25d012a4-e043-5863-a60e-562fbc6d4763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-2, x = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085641} {:answer/id #uuid "49ed6d3a-0c88-59f1-bad1-a0f0e26881dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085642} {:answer/id #uuid "515ba722-7f0f-5fde-bdb4-11ae120a46ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 0, x = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085643}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9a893397-b983-54ae-b23f-7a638107daf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085638}] :question/id #uuid "9c5d95ad-bb5b-5734-95d6-fffbd7781121" :question/math-academy-id "q-39796" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\frac{2}{2 - x} + \\frac{3}{2 + x}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3ecee87-11a8-5b31-9dca-3a44b18f83fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085759} {:answer/id #uuid "cd85c226-9ca4-5e6c-89fa-56a35f63ef57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 1, x = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085760} {:answer/id #uuid "296eff6c-9206-5823-bb93-0f28b35e920f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-1, x = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085761} {:answer/id #uuid "5b320f84-2893-5a99-b959-4cf809c16c6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085762} {:answer/id #uuid "6e876cfd-ddd5-5cc2-a627-8112dec7ef0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085763}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7f48246f-51c4-5662-886c-8db6c571a5c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085758}] :question/id #uuid "8fc376c7-6855-5bb9-8474-018bf5ce2776" :question/math-academy-id "q-4629" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\frac{3x^{2} - 12x}{x^{2} - 2x - 3}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30bf1417-e438-575a-b2ad-9a5df5d2c756" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 3, x = \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086299} {:answer/id #uuid "34051aff-0352-5996-91d8-59092638e892" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-12, x = 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086300} {:answer/id #uuid "5179640e-a535-5308-8b8d-8f7ce13b179b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-5, x = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086301} {:answer/id #uuid "5327d472-f83e-5dba-896d-945e7a11005c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-2, x = \\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086302} {:answer/id #uuid "7feffe94-3416-5a5f-9304-9e981d4f3453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x =-8, x = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086303}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8e7551a9-0422-54da-a740-462fe7d4546c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086298}] :question/id #uuid "300e7717-c412-5929-8eff-86d376ba1c7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6908" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x) = \\frac{1}{x^{2} + 6x - 16}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233720 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48d343cf-bcd6-550a-b491-b3a8f24b897d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48d343cf-bcd6-550a-b491-b3a8f24b897d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232435} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a58a3d7-2ee5-5f1e-bb4b-407526cca168" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232434} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8c830c3-be7a-5e3a-ae63-2642c7c3a58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8c830c3-be7a-5e3a-ae63-2642c7c3a58b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232437} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ec75490-1f9d-535a-8111-27b3e668b46e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232436}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5879d675-dcdb-5d51-8bbe-dbca569c522b" :question/math-academy-id "q-278249" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}(x-3)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(x-3)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=0$ and $x=3.$ \n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=0$ and $x=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234019 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34e39715-21d0-5ed5-8865-d1712b1e9cdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-39790" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{2{x}^{2}+x-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x+1}{(2x+3)(x-1)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+3)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "549a35d4-daa7-5446-a638-9c14ec7a2e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "549a35d4-daa7-5446-a638-9c14ec7a2e11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290993} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d774430-842a-541f-9061-499e5425b97e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291946} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c70ef40b-b3c8-5594-b1de-fc4a498090cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c70ef40b-b3c8-5594-b1de-fc4a498090cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291487} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4ec5b0d-a086-5286-b7b0-f076712442f1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292499}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "058c4194-dfaa-5b79-9c0a-c7395af3b9d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-269955" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{6{x}^{2}+9x}{{x}^{2}-4}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3x(2x+3)}{(x-2)(x+2)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-2$ and $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e26fefe4-4552-5e54-8943-082eca6e26f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e26fefe4-4552-5e54-8943-082eca6e26f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293821} :answer-field/id #uuid "390c6ae0-8d81-5b0d-8148-b7b8e853b57c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294123} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca33217-9f32-559d-8ee5-0dd07420ba0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ca33217-9f32-559d-8ee5-0dd07420ba0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292986} :answer-field/id #uuid "e24f017a-5ffe-50af-8304-ccc74a86bcb5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294726}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cdefa676-1018-5cf9-a9ee-3c8d0b2dc9e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-278534" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2}{x-1}+\\frac{1}{x+5}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nAdding both fractions and then factoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2}{x-1}+\\frac{1}{x+5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+5)+(x-1)}{(x-1)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+10+x-1}{(x-1)(x+5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3x+9}{(x-1)(x+5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-5$ and $x=1.$ \n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-5$ and $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "680b28ce-e155-5b00-b8b0-99b62cf2c874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324839} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3410ea-fc43-5f14-adf6-d8f0c968b533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324873} {:answer/id #uuid "834d459f-93be-51d4-a7ef-620128a21142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4,\\,x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325076} {:answer/id #uuid "d7774069-0d55-5431-b454-59b7537bb35f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=0,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325845} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf2746c-d5f4-5dd7-81ec-fe0ec7f75242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebf2746c-d5f4-5dd7-81ec-fe0ec7f75242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326048} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cf1b6f2-8390-50e9-8126-9a31d06519c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326298}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "347c5f9b-d82c-54af-b86f-728a645905de" :question/math-academy-id "q-39791" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}-8}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3}{2(x+2)(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-2$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "252db0d7-6b72-5535-bc72-42087bd9c168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345686} {:answer/id #uuid "3c61c17f-df35-5231-8f8c-9855ef44fbff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345862} {:answer/id #uuid "493990c6-7871-5af7-9742-efe497570105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345976} {:answer/id #uuid "7aebd289-0443-5cd1-8647-ef80a5d4ed52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346424} {:answer/id #uuid "e86bd07f-57d6-5d65-989c-5c3ebae8b75b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aebd289-0443-5cd1-8647-ef80a5d4ed52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346424} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0a0cf42-b725-56a9-a31d-6affed13eb8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347968}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45255a84-5494-574a-bd9f-880b4b37a48c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80669" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+3x}{{x}^{2}-3x-4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x(x+3)}{(x+1)(x-4)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-1$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-1$ and $x=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402688 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce1c8713-2cf3-5d3e-be72-a3135dfaed78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1,\\,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402690} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5c07fd-aef8-50a6-bb7a-0214e75d1fbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=1,\\,x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402691} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d397dd-0706-56c3-a545-4f1e11b5d1fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402692} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3acbb2-152b-5386-a5b3-d4d5f9778946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402693} {:answer/id #uuid "466aa8d5-5559-5bc5-8a5d-ddd22b7bb453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9d397dd-0706-56c3-a545-4f1e11b5d1fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3,\\,x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402692} :answer-field/id #uuid "aba4d313-1d11-58c4-bb5b-3d3a12220a0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402689}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca4d349e-c1ee-5a19-a1e0-b0c9e7e02dd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-39793" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x}{(x+3)(x+1)(x-1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)(x+1)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the three solutions $x=-3,$ $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are  $x=-3,\\,$ $x=-1$ and $x=1.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}]}
